WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.559
Great, now we have the theory out of the way, let's jump into the engine and have a look

2
00:00:06.559 --> 00:00:09.429
at some of these examples.

3
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:17.089
Now the first way of making a material interactive, this actually isn't going to

4
00:00:17.089 --> 00:00:24.309
require us to do any coding at all, except for within the material itself.

5
00:00:24.309 --> 00:00:26.890
Now let me demonstrate this to start off with.

6
00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:33.210
I'm coming up to example six here, camera distance. If I was to just press play,

7
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:42.490
now you would see here we have a bridge, a bridge which appears as the character moves across it.

8
00:00:43.799 --> 00:00:50.679
You can see here, we've got the bridge, you can see that as we get closer the bridge just moves

9
00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:59.210
into place, giving us this nice dynamic effect. Another example of the same sort of

10
00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:05.290
functionality working in the same way would be this door here. As I get close, the door opens,

11
00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:11.420
and as I get far away the door closes. Now this can be done in a number of different ways,

12
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:18.730
but what I'm using here, and I'll jump into the material, so what you can see here is I'm using

13
00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:23.930
primarily the world position offset. The only other thing I've got, I've got nothing else plugged in

14
00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:29.209
here really. I played around with the base color and a couple other odds and sorts, but the world

15
00:01:29.209 --> 00:01:35.849
position offset is the only thing that I'm really using. Now I am lerping between the A,

16
00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:43.209
so you must have come across a lerp by now, but what a lerp will do is, depending on the alpha,

17
00:01:43.209 --> 00:01:51.290
if the value that comes into the alpha is either going to provide the value from A,

18
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:56.250
if the alpha is zero, if the alpha is one, it will provide the value from B,

19
00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:02.010
and if the alpha is 0.5 it will provide halfway between these two values.

20
00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.409
So you can see what I'm doing here. World position offset. This allows me to move

21
00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:18.199
the vertices. If it's 0 and 1, it's going to be in its original position. However, if the value

22
00:02:18.199 --> 00:02:27.319
is at B, it's going to create a location far away. I've got a couple of rather more clever

23
00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:33.319
things I'm doing here. For example, I'm using the world position to generate a random value,

24
00:02:34.039 --> 00:02:39.960
meaning that each of the instances will have a different value, so they'll all move to a

25
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:46.659
different location. I'm adding some random rotation, and I'm using the sine wave to make

26
00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:55.750
this this bob up and down. Now what I'm doing here is this is plugging into the alpha,

27
00:02:56.389 --> 00:03:01.909
but I am looking at the distance between the object position and the camera position,

28
00:03:02.629 --> 00:03:07.430
getting the distance. I'm doing a little bit of maths and clamping it to 0 to 1 so it doesn't

29
00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:14.949
go over or under the values. And so as the camera gets closer to the object's position,

30
00:03:14.949 --> 00:03:19.669
it's going to provide us a different value between 0 and 1, and that's what will allow

31
00:03:19.669 --> 00:03:24.229
us to look. And so you can see here as I move further away in the viewport,

32
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:31.210
it moves off to a random location. So this is the sort of simplest example of it. We have

33
00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:36.009
all we've done in this large section is generated a bobbing up and down random location,

34
00:03:36.009 --> 00:03:42.520
and then based off the distance of the camera, we are moving between those two points. Now

35
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:50.280
we're effectively doing the same thing with the door, but it's a little bit more complicated.

36
00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:58.120
Do feel free to check out the material here in order to see how that works.
